/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a short element of a wall of thickness t (mm) carrying shear stress τ (N/mm²).
   - q is the shear flow (N/mm); τ is the shear stress in the wall (N/mm²); t is the wall thickness (mm).
   - Where does q come from?
   - q(s) is the shear flow at distance s along the wall from a free edge (N/mm), positive in the direction of increasing s; Sy is the shear force in the +y direction (N); Ixx is the second moment of area about the centroidal x axis (mm⁴); y is 
   - Three facts follow.
   - Each element q ds is a small force along the wall.
   - The shear centre is also called the flexural centre.
   - A channel has a web of depth h and two flanges of width b on one side.
   - e is the distance of the shear centre from the web mid-line, on the side away from the flanges (mm); h is the web depth and b the flange width, both on mid-lines (mm); t is the uniform thickness (mm).
   - The centroid of the same channel lies on the flange side of the web, so the centroid and the shear centre lie on opposite sides of the web.
*/
